33:Tomato is one of the major vegetable crops of the province of Balochistan, Pakistan. This province has been under extensive
36:as an amendment in agricultural lands reduces the absorption of heavy metals by crops from contaminated soil. In this study,
37:the soil was contaminated with coal mine tailing debris. A mixture of air-dried poultry manure with wood biochar or farmyard
38:manure biochar at the ratio of 1:1 w:w was amended in soil. For the treatment of thorough mixing of fertilizer in the soil,
39:biochar-based organic fertilizer was applied at the rates of 5 and 10 t ha -1 and the soil was contaminated with 10 t ha-1 of debris
40:from coal mine tailings. To assess the influence of fertilizer in heavily-contaminated soil, debris was placed in the root zone of
41:tomato at 166.5 t ha-1 rate and fertilizers were added at 33.3 and 66.6 t ha-1 rates, followed by their mixing with debris. The
42:fertilizers decreased the concentration of Cadmium (Cd) by 63 – 83% and arsenic (As) by 51 – 73% in tomato fruits than
43:control (P ≤ 0.05) in low-contaminated soil. In high-contaminated soil, fertilizers decreased the concentration of Cd by 40 -
44:49% and As by 44 – 56% in tomato fruits (P ≤ 0.05). A negative correlation was observed between the level of contamination in
46:concentration of heavy metals in fruits of tomato (R2 = 0.79; P < 0.01 for Cd and R2 = 0.27; P < 0.01 for As). The application of
47:poultry manure-mixed biochar in coal mine tailing contaminated soil has the potential for reducing the concentration of these
48:heavy metals in tomato fruits.
