SAFETY OF BLACK SOLDIER FLY LARVAE: MICROBIAL AND HEAVY METAL RISKS

Zuzana Barboráková*1, Jana Urminská2, Silvia Jakabová2, Eduard Kolesár3, Dana Tančinová1, Dana Urminská1, Eva Ivanišová2,
Marián Tomka1, Lukáš Hleba1, Monika Mrvová1, Juraj Medo1, Zuzana Mašková1
Address(es): Ing. Zuzana Barboráková, PhD.
1
  Slovak University of Agriculture in Nitra, Faculty of Biotechnology and Food Sciences, Institute of Biotechnology, Tr. A. Hlinku 2, 949 76 Nitra, Slovakia, phone
number: +421376414494.
2
  Slovak University of Agriculture in Nitra, Faculty of Biotechnology and Food Sciences, Institute of Food Sciences, Tr. A. Hlinku 2, 949 76 Nitra, Slovakia.
3
  ecol Trade, s.r.o., J. Haška 1, 94901 Nitra, Slovakia.

*Corresponding author: zuzana.barborakova@uniag.sk                                                                                       https://doi.org/10.55251/jmbfs.12069

ARTICLE INFO                    ABSTRACT

Received 13. 8. 2024            Black soldier fly larvae (BSFL) are considered a sustainable protein source and an effective means of recycling organic waste. They are
                                rich in nutrients, including proteins and lipids, making them suitable for animal feed and potentially for human consumption. In addition
Revised 18. 11. 2024
                                to their nutritional benefits, BSFL also have environmental benefits, i.e., reducing waste and greenhouse gas emissions. However, concerns
Accepted 25. 11. 2024           about their safety persist, particularly regarding contamination by pathogenic microorganisms, toxins, and heavy metals. The aim of our
Published 1. 12. 2024           study was to investigate the effect of different feedstuffs on heavy metal accumulation and on the microcenosis (Enterobacteriaceae,
                                aerobic bacterial endospores, microscopic filamentous fungi) of BSFL. BSFL were fed four feed treatments (I: egg pasta cooked in whole
Regular article                 milk; II: cooked rice with peas; III: poultry feed; and IV: cooked couscous, boiled eggs, and raw carrot peels). The results of our study
                                showed that feed variants significantly affect the microbial safety of BSFL. Using MALDI-TOF, we identified 13 species of family
                                Enterobacteriaceae in BSFL, including facultative pathogenic species Enterobacter cloacae, Klebsiella aerogenes, Klebsiella
                                pneumoniae, Morganella morganii, and Proteus mirabilis. Our research confirmed that BSFL fed different feed variants under
                                experimental laboratory conditions contained heavy metals, including cadmium, copper, manganese, molybdenum, nickel, lead, and zinc.
                                Cadmium concentrations in BSFL ranged from 0.12 to 0.18 mg.kg -1, with the highest values measured in BSFL in variant IV.

                                Keywords: Hermetia illucens, Microorganisms, Pathogens, Polyalloxides, Hazards


